Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-20
Subject: Guard pobjectdef(resulttype) in tccal first pass
License: expat

In compiler/tccal.pas the first pass of a method-call node does:

    (not(oo_has_virtual in
         pobjectdef(p^.methodpointer^.resulttype)^.objectoptions))

This reinterprets whatever `methodpointer^.resulttype' points at as
a tobjectdef and reads the `objectoptions' field from it.  That
field exists only on tobjectdef.  Its sibling types -- in particular
tclassrefdef -- are strictly smaller records with no shared
hierarchy and no `objectoptions'.

For `ClassName.Method(...)' call shapes the receiver's resulttype
is a tclassrefdef, so the cast is wrong by construction and the
field read runs past the end of that allocation.  On native FPC/x86
the four bytes past the tclassrefdef usually land in adjacent live
heap, so the smallset test returned some bit and the surrounding
logic tolerated either outcome.  Under the tp2cc-translated bootstrap the
allocation is sized exactly, and the oob read is visible.

Fix: guard the cast with `resulttype^.deftype=objectdef'.  When the
receiver is not an object instance, skip the virtual-method test
and fall through to `method_must_be_valid := true', which is the
conservative branch the original code already takes for every other
non-constructor case.

--- a/compiler/tccal.pas
+++ b/compiler/tccal.pas
@@ -1267,8 +1267,19 @@

                      { R.Assign is not a constructor !!! }
                      { but for R^.Assign, R must be valid !! }
+                     { The `pobjectdef(resulttype)^.objectoptions' read below
+                       type-puns whatever `resulttype' points at as a tobjectdef.
+                       That isn't always true: for `ClassName.Method(...)` shapes
+                       the methodpointer's resulttype is a tclassrefdef (no
+                       objectoptions field), and the unguarded read walks off
+                       the end of that allocation.  Guard the cast: only treat
+                       the method's receiver as potentially non-virtual when
+                       the receiver's def really is an object def.  If it
+                       isn't, fall through to `method_must_be_valid := true',
+                       which is the conservative branch. }
                      if (p^.procdefinition^.proctypeoption=potype_constructor) or
                         ((p^.methodpointer^.treetype=loadn) and
+                        (p^.methodpointer^.resulttype^.deftype=objectdef) and
                         (not(oo_has_virtual in pobjectdef(p^.methodpointer^.resulttype)^.objectoptions))) then
                        method_must_be_valid:=false
                      else
